introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do we translate abstract logic into instructions a machine can execute?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on the transition from designed algorithms to executable code. You will learn how to automate logic using programming constructs, exploring how computers store data (<strong>variables</strong>), manipulate it (<strong>operators</strong>), and handle errors (<strong>exceptions</strong>) to build robust software."
  journey: "You'll start by mastering <strong>data types</strong> and <strong>variables</strong> to store information. Then, you'll learn to manipulate text with <strong>string operations</strong>, control the flow of execution, and finally, how to make your programs \"crash-proof\" using <strong>exception handling</strong> and <strong>debugging</strong> techniques."

concepts:
  - title: "Variables & Data Storage"
    icon: "fa-database"
    content: |
      <p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a named location in memory used to store data that can change during execution. Think of it as a labeled box where you can put a value, take it out, or replace it.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Declaration:</strong> Telling the computer "I need a box named X".</li>
          <li><strong>Initialization:</strong> Putting the first value into that box.</li>
          <li><strong>Scope:</strong> Creating <em>Global</em> variables (visible everywhere) vs <em>Local</em> variables (visible only inside a function).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In Python, variables are dynamically typed, meaning the "box" changes shape to fit the data you put in it (integer, float, string, or boolean).</p>

  - title: "String Manipulation"
    icon: "fa-font"
    content: |
      <p>Text is stored as <strong>Strings</strong> — sequences of characters. You'll learn to manipulate them using:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Concatenation:</strong> Joining strings together (e.g., <code>"Hello" + " " + "World"</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Slicing:</strong> Extracting specific parts of a string (e.g., <code>text[0:3]</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Casting:</strong> Converting strings to numbers and vice-versa (e.g., <code>int("5")</code>).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Error Handling & Debugging"
    icon: "fa-bug"
    content: |
      <p>Programs rarely work perfectly the first time. You will learn to identify and fix three types of errors:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Syntax Errors:</strong> breaking the rules of the language (like bad grammar).</li>
          <li><strong>Runtime Errors:</strong> crashing during execution (e.g., dividing by zero).</li>
          <li><strong>Logic Errors:</strong> running without crashing but giving the wrong result.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We use <strong>try/except</strong> blocks to handle Runtime errors gracefully, and <strong>trace tables</strong> or <strong>breakpoints</strong> to hunt down Logic errors.</p>
      
      <div class="warning-box">
          <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> The Golden Rule</h4>
          <p><strong>"Garbage In, Garbage Out."</strong> If your program accepts invalid input, it will produce invalid output. Always validate user input!</p>
      </div>

terminology:
  - term: "Variable"
    definition: "A designated memory location that stores a value that can change during the execution of a program."
  - term: "Constant"
    definition: "A value that cannot be altered by the program during normal execution."
  - term: "Identifier"
    definition: "The name given to a variable, function, class, or other entity in the code."
  - term: "Declaration"
    definition: "Specifying the name and data type of a variable before it is used."
  - term: "Initialization"
    definition: "Assigning an initial value to a variable at the moment it is declared."
  - term: "Concatenation"
    definition: "The operation of joining two strings end-to-end."
  - term: "Slicing"
    definition: "Extracting a section (substring) from a string based on proper indices."
  - term: "Exception"
    definition: "An event that disrupts the normal flow of the program's instructions (e.g., DivisionByZero)."
  - term: "Debugging"
    definition: "The process of identifying and removing errors from computer hardware or software."
  - term: "Breakpoint"
    definition: "An intentional stopping or pausing place in a program, put in place for debugging purposes."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Assignment vs Equality"
    correction: "Confusion between = (assignment: 'make this equal to...') and == (comparison: 'is this equal to...?')."
  - mistake: "Global vs Local Scope"
    correction: "Believing a variable declared inside a function is accessible everywhere. It dies when the function ends!"
  - mistake: "Strings are Numbers"
    correction: "Thinking \"5\" + \"5\" equals 10. In programming, \"5\" + \"5\" equals \"55\" (concatenation)."

